<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cooch Behar Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1887</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Victor Jubilee Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cooch Behar Palace, also called the Victor Jubilee Palace, is a royal residence in Cooch Behar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, built in 1887 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nripendra-narayan">Nripendra Narayan</a> in a style inspired by the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and Buckingham Palace. A two-storey brick building with a domed central hall, it is now a museum run by the Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cooch-behar-palace.jpg" alt="Cooch Behar Palace" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kingshuk Mondal · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Cooch_Behar_Palace_or_the_Cooch_Behar_Rajbari,_Cooch_Behar_District,_West_Bengal,_India_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
